--TEST--
A string literal at a parameter typed `property<T>` alone that names no public property of `T` is the unknown-member error at the literal, and a string variable there still needs `as` — `rule:types/property-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A name that is not a public property of `Order` does not compile.
class Order {
    public string $id = "o1";
    private int $secret = 1;
}

class Report {
    public static function column(property<Order> $field): string {
        return "column " . ($field as string);
    }
}

// `idd` is misspelled.
echo Report::column("idd"), "\n";
// `secret` is private.
echo Report::column("secret"), "\n";
// A variable is not a literal, so it needs `as property<Order>`.
string $name = "id";
echo Report::column($name), "\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Order` has no public property `$idd`
   --> %s:15:21
   |
15 | echo Report::column("idd"), "\n";
   |                     ^^^^^ this name is checked here
   = help: `rule:types/property-key` decides a written-out operand where it is written, and a key's values are `Order`'s public declared properties: `$id`

error[E0405]: `Order` has no public property `$secret`
   --> %s:17:21
   |
17 | echo Report::column("secret"), "\n";
   |                     ^^^^^^^^ this name is checked here
   = help: `rule:types/property-key` decides a written-out operand where it is written, and a key's values are `Order`'s public declared properties: `$id`

error[E0401]: expected `property<Order>`, found `string`
   --> %s:20:21
   |
20 | echo Report::column($name), "\n";
   |                     ^^^^^ this is `string`

error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
